WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.411
Atomics are not only about indivisible updates.

2
00:00:02.411 --> 00:00:08.267
They also define ordering relationships that affect what one thread can observe after another thread performs work.

3
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:11.711
That is why memory ordering is part of atomic design.

4
00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:15.156
Sequential consistency is the easiest starting point for most teams.

5
00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:21.700
It gives a single global order for sequentially consistent atomic operations, which usually makes code and reviews more straightforward.

6
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.556
It may not be the fastest option in every case, but clarity is often the safer default.

7
00:00:27.556 --> 00:00:30.312
Acquire and release ordering are common coordination concepts.

8
00:00:30.312 --> 00:00:37.890
A release operation can publish prior writes, and an acquire operation can observe that publication when paired through the same coordination design.

9
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.990
This is often used for readiness or handoff patterns.

10
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.368
Relaxed ordering is narrower.

11
00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:50.290
It can be appropriate for independent metrics counters or values where the exact relationship to other memory is not being used for coordination.

12
00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:56.490
It is risky when used to communicate ownership, readiness, object lifetime, or state transitions without a clear design.

13
00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.901
Weaker ordering choices should require explicit justification.

14
00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:06.479
The reviewer should see why sequential consistency is not being used, what relationship is required, and what tests or documentation support the

15
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:06.824
decision.

16
00:01:06.824 --> 00:01:15.090
Memory-ordering mistakes can be hard to diagnose because the code may pass ordinary tests and fail only under different timing or hardware conditions.

17
00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.535
That uncertainty is a reason to keep atomic protocols small.

18
00:01:18.535 --> 00:01:26.113
A reviewable atomic design documents what is being ordered, which threads participate, what state is being published or observed, and why the

19
00:01:26.113 --> 00:01:27.491
selected ordering is sufficient.

20
00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:35.069
If the explanation is difficult to write, a mutex, condition variable, queue, or clearer ownership transfer may be the better engineering choice.

21
00:01:35.069 --> 00:01:43.680
The point is not to avoid weaker ordering forever; it is to reserve it for cases where the team can preserve the reasoning over time.
